<https://meta.linked.archi/archimate4/derivation> a dcat:Dataset;
  dcterms:title "Linked.Archi ArchiMate 4.0 Derivation Rules"@en;
  dcterms:description """SHACL rules (sh:SPARQLRule) implementing the ArchiMate 4.0 derivation
rules of Appendix B: DR1–DR8, whose derived relationships are valid in any model, and PDR1–PDR12,
whose derived relationships may hold. Each rule is the join its definition states; the restrictions of
B.4 and the order of strength are written into each rule. A
valid rule derives a statement: the direct triple, the qualified predicate and the resource, a
relationship like any other. A potential rule derives a proposition: the resource alone, which reifies
the triple (rdf:reifies) without asserting it, for the modeller to confirm by asserting the triple, with
its qualified predicate. Either way the resource carries its certainty (:Valid or :Potential),
the relationships it was derived from (prov:wasDerivedFrom) — the qualified relationships the rule
matched, the modeller's or derived — and the activity that generated it (prov:wasGeneratedBy): the
derivation activity, the rule applied to them, whose plan is the rule (prov:qualifiedAssociation,
prov:hadPlan). Each rule is a prov:Plan. What runs the rules — an application, a pipeline — records
its run: an activity of its own, with its agent and when it ran, of which each derivation activity it
produced is part (dcterms:isPartOf). What the modeller stated is told from what a rule derived by the
graph, in a store (a derivation is kept in .../graph/derived), and by the resource. Before the rules run,
:QualifyRelationships gives each relationship the model states only as a direct triple a resource of
its own. The IRIs are in the model's namespace, in the Linked.Archi IRI layout (DD-41): a derivation is a
relationship, relationship/{digest} of its rule and premises, with its derivation activity and the
association as its parts, .../activity and .../activity/association; a qualified direct triple is
relationship/{digest} of the triple. A digest is the first 20 hex characters of the SHA-256 of what it is
of, joined by spaces. With no model loaded, or several, in urn:linked-archi:. The rules are SPARQL 1.2. A rule
derives only from relationships whose triple is asserted, in the model or by a valid derivation
— one that is only :Potential is for the modeller to confirm, as is any other proposition — so the rules give the closure of valid
derivations and one layer of potential ones; a valid derivation of a relationship that so far is only
:Potential makes it :Valid. A potential rule derives only what the relationship tables of Appendix B.5
permit between the two element types, as archimate4-relationship-tables.ttl gives them: load it with
the model.
Generated from Appendix B (inline style)."""@en;
  dcterms:conformsTo <http://www.w3.org/ns/shacl#>;
  vann:preferredNamespaceUri "https://meta.linked.archi/archimate4/derivation#";
  vann:preferredNamespacePrefix "am4deriv";
  owl:versionInfo "4.0";
  dcterms:modified "2026-11-19"^^xsd:date;
  adms:status <http://purl.org/adms/status/UnderDevelopment>;
  dcterms:license <http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/>;
  dcterms:creator [ a foaf:Person;
      foaf:name "Kalin Maldzhanski"
    ];
  dcat:theme <https://meta.linked.archi/catalog#languages>;
  dcat:keyword "ArchiMate 4.0"@en;
  dcat:landingPage <https://meta.linked.archi/archimate4/derivation/>;
  dcat:distribution [ a dcat:Distribution;
      dcat:accessURL <https://meta.linked.archi/archimate4/derivation/archimate4-derivation-rules.ttl>;
      dcat:downloadURL <https://meta.linked.archi/archimate4/derivation/archimate4-derivation-rules.ttl>;
      dcat:mediaType <http://www.iana.org/assignments/media-types/text/turtle>
    ] .

<https://meta.linked.archi/archimate4/element-shapes> a dcat:Dataset;
  dcterms:title "Linked.Archi ArchiMate 4.0 Element & Metamodel Shapes"@en;
  dcterms:description """SHACL shapes for validating ArchiMate 4.0 element instances and
the metamodel patterns the relationship tables cannot express: element names, self-loops
and cycles of composition and aggregation, a part in one whole only, attribute values,
